task: bench-spark-streaming-and-tuning
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/spark-streaming-and-tuning
worktree: ../okay-wt-ss-tuning

why: two asks from the operator, 2026-09-10, and they belong in one
  lane because both change every number in §20's tables:
  (1) Spark with its OWN event-time window — `withWatermark` +
      `window()` in Structured Streaming — instead of the batch RDD
      rendering, so the comparison with Flink is engine-to-engine
      rather than engine-to-batch;
  (2) every other lane checked for its BEST shape and its bottlenecks
      removed, so that the table prices the machinery and not our
      choice of accumulator.

what, in order:
  - the classpath question first, because it decides the architecture:
    does `SparkSession` start in okay-flink's ordinary Scala 3.9 test
    JVM, or does it need okay-spark's two-stdlib hack? Measured, not
    assumed. If it needs the hack, the benchmark moves to its own
    project rather than the hack moving onto the other lanes.
  - `Job.stats` flattened: the algebraic `count zip sum zip max`
    allocates THREE objects per element per pane (two tuples and an
    Option) and every lane pays it. One flat accumulator, asserted
    equal to the algebraic one, and the difference measured.
  - each competitor in its best shape: chunk-level folds for fs2, ZIO
    and kyo where their API has one; a secondary sort
    (`repartitionAndSortWithinPartitions`) instead of `groupByKey` for
    Spark's bunching stage, which is what its 3 GB peak is.
  - one final measurement run for both tables.

predict: the flat accumulator is worth more than any per-engine tweak
  (nine allocations per element in the sliding stage today), and it
  moves every lane by the same factor, so the RATIOS barely change —
  which is itself the point worth reporting.
